## SOLID and DRY Principles

### Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

Each class should have a single responsibility or reason to change.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// Entity handles only domain logic
export class Booking extends AggregateRoot<BookingProps, Uuid> {
  cancel(): void {
    // Domain logic only
  }
}

// Repository handles only persistence
export class MongoBookingRepository implements BookingRepository {
  async save(booking: Booking): Promise<void> {
    // Persistence logic only
  }
}

// Use case orchestrates the operation
export class CancelBookingUseCase {
  async execute(input: CancelBookingDto): Promise<BookingResponseDto> {
    // Orchestration only
  }
}
```

### Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// Base use case interface allows extension
export interface UseCase<Input, Output> {
  execute(input: Input): Promise<Output>;
}

// New use cases extend without modifying existing code
export class CreateBookingUseCase implements UseCase<CreateBookingDto, BookingResponseDto> {
  // Implementation
}

export class CancelBookingUseCase implements UseCase<CancelBookingDto, BookingResponseDto> {
  // Implementation
}
```

### Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

Objects of a derived class should be replaceable with objects of the base class.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// All entities can be treated uniformly
abstract class Entity<T, IdType> {
  equals(other?: Entity<T, IdType>): boolean { /* ... */ }
}

class Room extends Entity<RoomProps, Uuid> { /* ... */ }
class Booking extends AggregateRoot<BookingProps, Uuid> { /* ... */ }

// Both can be compared using equals()
```

### Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

Many specific interfaces are better than a single general interface.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// Specific repository interface per aggregate
export interface BookingRepository {
  findById(id: Uuid): Promise<Booking | null>;
  save(booking: Booking): Promise<void>;
}

export interface RoomRepository {
  findById(id: Uuid): Promise<Room | null>;
  findAvailable(dateRange: DateRange): Promise<Room[]>;
}

// Generic base for common operations
export interface Repository<T, IdType> {
  findById(id: IdType): Promise<T | null>;
  save(entity: T): Promise<void>;
}
```

### Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// Use case depends on abstraction (repository interface)
export class CreateBookingUseCase {
  constructor(
    private readonly bookingRepository: BookingRepository,  // Abstraction
    private readonly roomRepository: RoomRepository         // Abstraction
  ) {}
}

// Infrastructure implements the abstraction
export class MongoBookingRepository implements BookingRepository {
  // Implementation details
}
```

### DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)

Each piece of knowledge should have a single, unambiguous, authoritative representation.

**Good Example:**
```typescript
// Base classes eliminate duplication
export abstract class Entity<T, IdType extends Identifier<unknown>> {
  protected readonly _id: IdType;
  protected props: T;

  equals(other?: Entity<T, IdType>): boolean {
    // Single implementation for all entities
  }
}

// Shared value objects avoid repetition
export class Uuid extends Identifier<string> {
  static generate(): Uuid {
    return new Uuid(randomUUID());
  }
}
```

